    <comp lib="11" loc="(20,1080)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#87eb675e"/>
      <a name="format" val="markdownish"/>
      <a name="style" val="margin: 0.5em; h1 { accent: underline 0.25em; }; h2 { accent: block 0.75em #3dd;}"/>
      <a name="text">## XOR &amp; XNOR Multiple-Input Behavior
Multi-input XOR and XNOR gates can be confusing. Normally, XOR outputs a 1 when an *odd number of inputs* are 1, and XNOR does the opposite. This "parity" behavior is most typical. For XOR and XNOR gates with more than two inputs, the ***multiple-input behavior*** attribute allows for a different, less common behavior, where XOR outputs a 1 when *exactly one input* is a 1, and XNOR does the opposite. 

**Suggestion** &#128073; Don't use multi-input XOR and XNOR gates. For parity calculations, use the ***Even Parity*** or ***Odd Parity*** gates instead. For *exactly-one* calculations, which are less common, consider building a circuit from a ***Bit Adder*** and a ***Comparator*** (found under ***Arithmetic***).

## Errors, Unconnected, and Floating Inputs

As a convenience, any input left unconnected is ignored: if there is no wire or other connection to the input, the simulator acts as if the input did not exist at all. So a 5-input gate with only two inputs connected behaves just like a 2-input gate. This doesn't match physical circuits or more realistic simulators, where such inputs would be considered *floating*, but it makes editing a circuit a little more forgiving. If all inputs are unconnected, the output is an error. If only one input is connected, the behavior depends on the gate type. To enable a more strict simulation behavior, treating unconnected inputs as floating values, go to ***Project &gt; Options &gt; Simulation***.

</a>
      <a name="textwidth" val="480"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="11" loc="(20,1650)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#87eb675e"/>
      <a name="format" val="markdownish"/>
      <a name="style" val="margin: 0.5em; h1 { accent: underline 0.25em; }; h2 { accent: block 0.75em #3dd;}"/>
      <a name="text">## Output Driver Option

Normally, gates use **push-pull** outputs: they actively output either 1 or 0 at all times, as long as the inputs are well defined. The ***Output Driver*** attribute allows for two other behaviors. 
* With a **pull-up-only** output driver (equivalent to open-emitter/open-source) a gate can actively output 1, but instead of outputting 0 it will produce a floating (z) output. 
* With **pull-down-only** output driver (equivalent to open-collector/open-drain), a gate can actively output 0, but instead of outputting 1 it will produce a floating (z) output. 
This allows creation of *wired-OR* and *wired-AND* busses. ***Pull Resistor*** (found in the ***Analog*** library) can be useful for these too.</a>
      <a name="textwidth" val="480"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="11" loc="(20,20)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#87eb675e"/>
      <a name="format" val="markdownish"/>
      <a name="style" val="margin: 0.5em; h1 { accent: underline 0.25em; }; h2 { accent: block 0.75em #3dd;}"/>
      <a name="text"># Overview of Common Gates
Common mutliple-input logic gates are AND, OR, NAND, NOR, XOR, XNOR.

Each one takes several inputs, combines these using the rules of Boolean logic, and outputs the result.</a>
      <a name="textwidth" val="480"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="11" loc="(20,2230)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#87eb675e"/>
      <a name="format" val="markdownish"/>
      <a name="style" val="margin: 0.5em; h1 { accent: underline 0.25em; }; h2 { accent: block 0.75em #3dd;}"/>
      <a name="text">## Gate Shapes and International Standards

Different standards bodies have adopted different shapes for  common gates. In **Logisim &gt; Settings &gt; International** you can choose from **ANSI/IEEE Standard 91a-1991** (curved, distinctive shapes commonly used in computer science textbooks and educational materials), **IEC 60617-12** (rectangular, uniform shapes often used in professional settings and engineering), and **DIN 40700** (an older, less common standard).</a>
      <a name="textwidth" val="480"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="11" loc="(20,380)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#87eb675e"/>
      <a name="format" val="markdownish"/>
      <a name="style" val="margin: 0.5em; h1 { accent: underline 0.25em; }; h2 { accent: block 0.75em #3dd;}"/>
      <a name="text">## Multiple Inputs and Multi-Bit Gates
Use the ***number of inputs*** attribute to allow more than two inputs. All inputs are combined using the usual rules of Boolean logic to produce a single output result. Use the *data bits* attribute to allow a gate to operate on multiple bits simultaneously.

**Keyboard shortcut** &#128073;To change the *number of inputs* for a gate, select it and type a number. Or select several gates to change them all.

**Keyboard shortcut** &#128073;To change the *data bits*, select some gates, hold the option key (⌥) or Alt key, and type a number.
</a>
      <a name="textwidth" val="480"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="11" loc="(20,740)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#87eb675e"/>
      <a name="format" val="markdownish"/>
      <a name="style" val="margin: 0.5em; h1 { accent: underline 0.25em; }; h2 { accent: block 0.75em #3dd;}"/>
      <a name="text">## Visuals
The ***gate size*** attribute allows a choice of *narrow*, *medium*, or *wide*. Gates can be rotated to face any direction using the ***facing*** attribute. A gate can be given a ***label***, which will follow the gate if it is moved or copied.

**Keyboard shortcut** &#128073;Select some gates, then use arrow keys (↑→↓←) to rotate them so they face a different direction.

## Negated Inputs
Any of the inputs can be configured to be active-low. This behaves exactly as if there were a NOT gate attached to that input, but used judiciously, negated inputs can make circuits more compact and easier to undertand.</a>
      <a name="textwidth" val="480"/>
    </comp>
